<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sweyn Forkbeard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 960–<a href="/years/1014">1014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Sweyn I of Denmark</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sweyn Forkbeard (c. 960–1014) was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> from about 986, after rebelling against his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a>, and from 1000 also overlord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. He led repeated raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and in 1013 conquered it and was accepted as king, but died in February 1014. His son Cnut later won England and built the North Sea Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard.jpg" alt="Sweyn Forkbeard" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ole Ryhl Olsson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sweyn_Forkbeard_by_L._Fr%C3%B8lich_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sweyn was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a> and an unknown mother. Harald had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the mid-960s and extended Danish royal authority. In 986 or 987 Sweyn revolted against him with the support of sections of the nobility and seized the Danish throne. Harald was defeated and wounded in battle, driven into exile and soon died of his wounds. The historian Timothy Bolton describes a brief civil war that ended in a scandalous regicide and left a deep scar on Danish memory.</p><p>Sweyn extended Danish control east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a> into Skane, and the main centres of power shifted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a>. Secure at home, he became the first Scandinavian king known to have led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, wealthy and militarily weak under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a>, was the main target, and the tribute Sweyn extracted extended his power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 994 Sweyn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> without success and raided the south-east until the English paid a tribute of 16,000 pounds. King Æthelred persuaded Olaf to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> to challenge Sweyn's overlordship, and many Norwegian magnates recognised Olaf as king. In 999 or 1000 Sweyn defeated him at the naval Battle of Svold and appointed the sons of Hakon Sigurdsson as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarls</a>. He returned to England in 1003, sacked Exeter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thetford">Thetford</a> and Norwich, until a severe famine forced him back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1005.</p><p>In August 1013 he came back with his son Cnut, intent on conquest, landing at Gainsborough. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> of the East Midlands and the North submitted, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> the whole nation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> included, accepted him as king. Æthelred spent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a> and fled to Richard, Duke of Normandy. Historians disagree whether Sweyn was a sincere Christian; he continued the Christianisation begun by his father, with churches built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sweyn died at Gainsborough on 3 February 1014 and was buried at York; according to Thietmar a woman had the body exhumed and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Most historians say he lay at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a>, but an empty grave found at the Holy Trinity church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> has been proposed as his. After his death the Danish fleet chose Cnut as king, but the English recalled Æthelred, who drove Cnut out. Medieval English hagiography made Sweyn a villain killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-the-martyr">Edmund the Martyr</a>, while modern historians describe a great warrior and a very successful king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Swein Forkbeard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Harald also built the bridge at Ravning Enge and the circular fortresses of Trelleborg, Fyrkat and Aggersborg, which tree-ring dating places around 980; according to Adam of Bremen his son <b>Sweyn Forkbeard</b> rebelled and drove him into exile, where he died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Viking kings, the last of them Eric Bloodaxe, ruled York at intervals until 954, and under Æthelred the Unready renewed Danish attacks led to the conquest of England by <b>Sweyn Forkbeard</b> in 1013 and by his son Cnut in 1016.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A second wave of attacks from the 980s brought heavy tributes, the Danegeld, and the conquest of England by <b>Sweyn Forkbeard</b> in 1013; his son Cnut the Great ruled England, the Kingdom of Denmark and, from 1028, the Kingdom of Norway.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>No throne was safe: Harald Bluetooth was overthrown by his own son, <b>Sweyn Forkbeard</b>, and Olaf Tryggvason was killed in battle in 1000.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li><li><blockquote>Victims reputedly included the sister of <b>Sweyn Forkbeard</b>, who launched repeated invasions and took the English crown in 1013.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-13">13 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In a large naval battle in the western Baltic, King Olaf Tryggvason of Norway was defeated and killed by an alliance of <b>Sweyn Forkbeard</b> of Denmark, Olof Skötkonung of Sweden and Eric Haakonsson, Earl of Lade.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-9">9 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in Gainsborough, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56,10&amp;year=995" aria-label="Denmark on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4767 256 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sweyn Forkbeard"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5217 526h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5217 526h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56,10&amp;year=995">What was here in 995 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
